<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · text</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eight-legged essay</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1480s–<a href="/years/1905">1905</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a> · Also known as Baguwen</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The eight-legged essay was the style of essay written in the imperial examinations of the Ming and Qing dynasties in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>, intended to test candidates' merits for government service through Confucian thought and knowledge of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/four-books-and-five-classics">Four Books and Five Classics</a>. Its eight legs were the sections of a fixed structure, from breaking open the topic to the conclusion, with rules for the number of sentences and words. The term first appeared in the early reign of the Chenghua Emperor, and the form was required in the examinations from the 1480s. Widely criticised under the Qing for its rigid, formulaic character, it was permanently abolished in 1905 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guangxu-emperor">Guangxu Emperor</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eight-legged-essay.jpg" alt="Eight-legged essay" width="330" height="186" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 三猎 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:状元卷.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The form probably grew out of Jingyi, a rhetorical form made popular in the eleventh century by the reformer and poet <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wang-anshi">Wang Anshi</a> (1021–1086). This led to the four-part pattern called qǐchéngzhuǎnhé, which in turn developed into the eight-legged essay under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ming-empire">Ming dynasty</a>. In the 17th century the scholar Gu Yanwu wrote that the form had become standard in the 15th century, when its eight parts were fixed.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Contents</h2><p>Candidates wrote under a set title and a set rhyming word, around which the essay or poem had to rhyme. They were advised to avoid offensive vocabulary and any clue to their own identity or social status. The pattern allowed some variation: a short transitional leg, the xùgǔ, could be added before the central leg, and the final leg could be left out.</p><p>One master of the form was Wang Ao (1450–1524), whose essay on the topic If the people enjoy sufficiency how could the ruler suffer from insufficiency? argues that a ruler is rich when his people are, so taxes need not be raised. Tang Shunzi took first place in the examination of 1529 with an essay on a passage of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mencius">Mencius</a> about the thinker Zi Mo, who held to the middle course between the teachings of Yang Zi and Mo Zi.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reception and legacy</h2><p>Because success in the examinations depended on the form, Ming printers sold collections of successful essays as guides. The first, in the 16th century, were pirated, but in 1587 the government suggested reprinting the best essays of the previous century as models. Examination papers were also printed with the graders' comments in order of rank, and the spread of such books drew more candidates to the examinations.</p><p>Defenders noted that the fixed structure made a vast number of papers easier and fairer to grade, and the scholar Tian Qilin argued that it represented a complex literary tradition. As early as the 17th century critics blamed it for the decline of classical poetry and prose: Wu Qiao wrote that people exhausted themselves on the essay and wrote poetry only with their spare energy, and the philosopher Huang Zongxi agreed. Early in the Kangxi reign the Four Regents revoked the form, though the order was later repealed, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qianlong-emperor">Qianlong Emperor</a> said he could not understand many candidates' essays. Some modern Chinese critics hold that it narrowed innovative thinking and contributed to cultural stagnation in the 19th century.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eight-legged essay” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
